A returned demonstration must be analyzed before the next puzzle action; it is not a cue to request another. For each pair, record the input/output dimensions and determine whether the output is a crop, transformed subgrid, summary, or full-grid result. Then identify how the output is derived and check the candidate rule against every pair received so far. For large grids, use a structured diff or systematically compare candidate subwindows/patterns rather than relying on visual impression. Apply the rule to the current test and identify whether competing hypotheses predict different outputs. Submit when the rule is sufficiently supported; request another demonstration only when a named unresolved choice would change the test prediction. Do not spend demos merely to postpone analysis, and do not substitute unrelated or no-op tool calls for the pairwise reasoning. Strict action-JSON format can still be preserved: this gate is internal, not user-facing narration.

